1.0 Copyleft-Protected Software Defined

"Source form": A form of computer program source code, as used by a
computer system to generate an executable file (or other derivative work),
where the exact changes made to the source code are reflected in any
derived works.  The source form is not only text files with human-readable
content, but also binary object files and libraries that can be converted
to source code by standard tools and processes.

The source form must include all files and directories required for
compilation of the software, including configuration files, scripts,
documentation, etc.  If a particular piece of software contains only one
file in its source form (for example, a single library), then this file
shall be considered as an entire "work" under this License.

If any file or portion of source code is not included with the object
code (or other derivative work) for distribution, then that file shall
be made available to recipients from a location specified by the author
of the software, and such location must be provided in the documentation
distributed with the software.

1.1 Source Code Availability

The "Source Form" of a Program means all the information and statements
used to define a program, including any accompanying documentation.  This
includes:

a) The text form of the source code
b) Any associated files (such as configuration files, scripts, etc.)
c) Documentation for how to install, compile, and run the program
d) A list of all dependencies and their licenses
e) Any additional information needed to create derived works

1.2 Network Use Defined

"Network use" means the act of providing or accessing a network service
that is based on a Program.  This includes:

a) Hosting the program for others to access via the internet
b) Providing the program as an API endpoint
c) Accessing the program remotely through HTTP, WebSocket, or other protocols
d) Any use where users interact with the program over a network

