            HOW TO APPLY THESE TERMS AND CONDITIONS TO YOUR PROGRAM

1. A copyright notice stating who holds the rights to the program and what year
   it was first published.  The notice must be clear and conspicuous.

2. The text of this License document, or a link to it from a website, is not
   sufficient.  You must either:

a) Include the full text of this License with your distribution, or
b) Clearly indicate where the license text can be found (e.g., in the
   program's source code repository, documentation, or README file), AND include
   a direct URL to the license text at https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html

3. A notice stating that the Program is distributed under these terms and conditions,
   including:

a) The name of the Program,
b) A description of what the Program does,
c) Any copyright notices or patent disclosures,
d) The license under which the Program is provided.

4. If the Program includes a disclaimer of warranty as described in section 6,
   you must include that text with your distribution.

5. You may not impose additional restrictions on recipients beyond those listed
   in these Terms and Conditions.

                    HOW TO APPLY THESE TERMS AND CONDITIONS TO DERIVATIVE WORKS

1. A derivative work is a work based on the Program, including but not limited to:

a) Modifications or improvements made to the Program
b) Derivatives of the Program (including "patches," "forks," etc.)
c) Modifications of the Program that create new functionality or features
d) Programs that combine the Program with other code (including linking)
e) Any work that incorporates all or part of the Program in a modified form

2. A derivative work is subject to these Terms and Conditions, meaning:

a) You must license the entire derivative work under this License
b) You cannot impose additional restrictions on recipients
c) You may not exclude any terms or conditions from this License
d) You cannot restrict how users modify or distribute the Program

3. Exception for Independent Works

You may create a derivative work that combines the Program with other code,
provided that:

a) The independent work is distributed under its own terms (as long as those
   terms are compatible with this License), and
b) You clearly indicate which parts of the derivative work are derived from
   the Program.

To comply with section 3, you must either:

a) Clearly mark all modifications made to the Program, or
b) Include a separate file (or files) that list all changes made to the
   Program and state clearly how these relate to the original Program.

4. Exception for Bug Fixes

If you only make bug fixes to the Program, those fixes are not considered
derivative works under this License, provided that:

a) The fix does not introduce new functionality or features, or
b) The fix is a simple patch that does not significantly change the nature of the
   Program.

However, if your "bug fix" introduces new functionality or significantly changes
the Program's behavior, it may be considered a derivative work and thus subject
to this License.

5. Exception for Library Code

You may distribute a program that links to the Program (whether as a library
or executable) under the terms of another license (other than GPL), provided
that:

a) The linking is necessary for the combined work to function properly, and
b) The combined work does not contain any significant modifications to the
   Program itself.

